<section class="st" style="--lc:var(--y)"><p class="lead">Jaipur, the capital of Rajasthan, is known as the Pink City for the rose-coloured buildings of its old quarter. It has hilltop forts, royal palaces, busy bazaars and some of India’s best craft shopping.</p>
<p>Together with <a href="/India/delhi">Delhi</a> and <a href="/India/agra">Agra</a>, Jaipur makes up the Golden Triangle, the most popular first-time route through India.</p>
<p>This guide covers when to go, what to see, how to get there from Delhi, where to stay and how to plan two days.</p></section>

<section class="st" id="season" style="--lc:var(--gr)"><h2>Best Time to Visit Jaipur</h2>
<p>October to March is the best time to visit Jaipur, when days are warm and nights are cool.</p>
<ol class="mo" aria-label="Comfort level by month"><li style="--k:#8CCBA6">Jan</li><li style="--k:#8CCBA6">Feb</li><li style="--k:#8CCBA6">Mar</li><li style="--k:var(--or)">Apr</li><li style="--k:var(--or)">May</li><li style="--k:var(--or)">Jun</li><li style="--k:#6FA3E8">Jul</li><li style="--k:#6FA3E8">Aug</li><li style="--k:#6FA3E8">Sep</li><li style="--k:var(--gr)">Oct</li><li style="--k:var(--gr)">Nov</li><li style="--k:#8CCBA6">Dec</li></ol>
<div class="key"><span><i style="background:var(--gr)"></i>Most pleasant</span><span><i style="background:#8CCBA6"></i>Cool, good for sightseeing</span><span><i style="background:var(--or)"></i>Very hot</span><span><i style="background:#6FA3E8"></i>Monsoon</span></div>
<div class="seas"><div><h3>October to March</h3><p>Warm days and cool nights, the best period for forts and palaces.</p></div><div><h3>December to January</h3><p>Evenings can be chilly, so pack a light jacket.</p></div><div><h3>April to June</h3><p>Very hot, often above 40°C. Keep sightseeing to early morning and late afternoon.</p></div><div><h3>July to September</h3><p>Monsoon rain, greener hills and fewer tourists.</p></div></div>
<div class="note">Festivals such as Diwali and the Jaipur Literature Festival in winter draw crowds, so book hotels early.</div></section>

<section class="st" id="sights" style="--lc:var(--bl)"><h2>Top Tourist Attractions in Jaipur</h2>
<p>Hill forts, royal palaces, an observatory and a lake palace.</p>
<ul class="stops">
<li><i class="pic" style="--c:var(--rd)"><svg><use href="#fort"/></svg></i><div><h3>Amber Fort<span class="tag">UNESCO</span></h3><p>On a hill about 11 km from the centre, part of the Hill Forts of Rajasthan. Its courtyards, mirrored halls and ramparts have wide views over the valley.</p></div></li>
<li><i class="pic" style="--c:var(--pk)"><svg><use href="#akshar"/></svg></i><div><h3>City Palace</h3><p>The royal family still lives in part of this complex. The rest is a museum of textiles, weapons and courtyards.</p></div></li>
<li><i class="pic" style="--c:var(--or)"><svg><use href="#gate"/></svg></i><div><h3>Hawa Mahal</h3><p>The Palace of Winds has a facade of hundreds of small windows. See it from the street in morning light, or from a café opposite.</p></div></li>
<li><i class="pic" style="--c:var(--gr)"><svg><use href="#minar"/></svg></i><div><h3>Jantar Mantar<span class="tag">UNESCO</span></h3><p>An observatory from the early 1700s with large stone instruments for tracking the sun, stars and time.</p></div></li>
<li><i class="pic" style="--c:var(--bl)"><svg><use href="#tomb"/></svg></i><div><h3>Jal Mahal</h3><p>A palace in the middle of Man Sagar Lake. Best seen from the roadside promenade, as visitors can’t normally go inside.</p></div></li>
